<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.8: Transformations of Functions</title>
    <p><alert>MTH 124</alert></p>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Vertical Translation of a Function</title>

        <p>
            The transformation <m>y=g(x)=f(x)+a</m> is a
            <em>vertical translation</em> of the graph of <m>f</m>.
            That is, every point <m>(x,f(x))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m> gets
            shifted vertically to the corresponding point <m>(x,f(x)+a)</m> on
            the graph of <m>g</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p workspace="0.15in">
        That is, <m>f(x)+a</m> shifts <m>f(x)</m> <fillin/>
        (up?/down?/right?/left?) by <fillin/>.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Horizontal Translation of a Function</title>

        <p>
            The transformation <m>y=h(x)=f(x-b)</m> is a
            <em>horizontal translation</em> of the graph of <m>f</m>.
            That is, every point <m>(x,f(x))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m> gets
            shifted horizontally to the corresponding point <m>(x+b,f(x))</m> on
            the graph of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p workspace="0.15in">
        That is, <m>f(x-b)</m> shifts <m>f(x)</m> <fillin/> by <fillin/>.
        **It's <term>opposite</term> the direction you probably think it moves!
        Ex: <m>f(x-2)</m> moves <term>right</term> by 2!**
    </p>

    <p workspace="0.05in">
        The graph on the left shows the vertical translation of the function
        <m>{y=f(x)=|x|}</m>, it is shifted vertically downward by 3, so it becomes
        the graph <m>{g(x) = f(x) - 3}</m>.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="30% 35% 30%">
      <image>
        <shortdescription>A piecewise graph f(x) shifted down by 3 under the transformation g(x)=f(x)-3.</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-0-a">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x) = abs(x)
                            </definition>
                            <definition>g(x)=f(x)-3</definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <graph function="f" dash="4 4" />
                                <graph function="g" />
                                <point p="(-3,3)" alignment="se" style="diamond"></point>
                                  <point p="(-3,0)" alignment="se" style="diamond"></point>
                                    <point p="(2,2)" alignment="se" style="box"></point>
                                      <point p="(2,-1)" alignment="se" style="box"></point>
                                <point p="(0,0)" alignment="se"><m>(0,0)</m></point>
                                <point p="(0,-3)" alignment="se" ><m>(0,-3)</m></point>
                                <vector tail="(-3,3)" v="(0,-3)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                                <vector tail="(3,3)" v="(0,-3)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                                <label anchor="(-2,2)" alignment="ne"><m>f(x)</m></label>
                                <label anchor="(-2,-1)" alignment="sw"><m>g(x)</m></label>

                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
      </image>
       <p>
        The graph on the right shows the horizontal translation of a function
        <m>f(x)</m> (given by a piecewise linear equation).
        Since <m>f</m> is shifted horizontally to the right by <m>2</m>, so it
        becomes the graph <m>{h(x) = f(x-2)}</m>.
    </p>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>A piecewise graph f(x) shifted right by 2 under the transformation h(x)=f(x-2).</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-0-b">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x) = 2*(x+2)+1
                            </definition>
                            <definition>g(x)=1/2*(x+2)+1</definition>
                            <definition>h(x)=f(x-2)</definition>
                            <definition>k(x)=g(x-2)</definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <graph function="f" dash="4 4" domain="(-5,-2)"/>
                                <graph function="g" dash="4 4" domain="(-2,5)"/>
                                <graph function="h" domain="(-5,0)"/>
                                <graph function="k"  domain="(0,5)"/>
                                <point p="(-2,1)" alignment="n"><m>(-2,1)</m></point>
                                <point p="(0,1)" alignment="se" ><m>(0,1)</m></point>
                                <point p="(-3.5,-2)" alignment="se" style="diamond"></point>
                                  <point p="(-1.5,-2)" style="diamond"/>
                                    <point p="(0,2)" style="box"/>
                                      <point p="(2,2)" style="box" />
                                <vector tail="(-3,-1)" v="(2,0)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                                <vector tail="(2,3)" v="(2,0)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                                <label anchor="(0,2.5)" alignment="ne"><m>f(x)</m></label>
                                <label anchor="(0,-2)" alignment="sw"><m>h(x)</m></label>

                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
    </image>

    </sidebyside>
   

    <p>
        Can plot the original, pick an individual point, transform it, pick
        another, ....
        until you see the pattern.
    </p>

