    <paragraphs>
        <title>Combining Functions (Section 1.9)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
        Note we can also combine two functions by adding, subtracting,
        multiplying, or even dividing functions, and this will create a new
        function.
        For instance if <m>f(x) = 3x + 1</m> and <m>g(x) = 2x - 4</m>, then we
        can take a new function <m>h(x)</m> to be the sum of <m>f</m> and
        <m>g</m>, so
        <md>
            h(x) = f(x) + g(x) = (3x + 1) + (2x - 4) = 5x - 3.
        </md>
        We can also subtract, multiply, and divide.
        For example we may choose to make a new function
        <m>k(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}</m>, which we could simplify in this case to
        <md>
            k(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \frac{3x+1}{2x-4}.
        </md>
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.9.1</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> defined by the
                table below and the piecewise linear functions <m>p</m> and
                <m>q</m> defined by the graph below.
                Assume that the lines in the figure pass through whole number
                coordinates where they appear to do so; for example,
                <m>(2,2)</m> lies on the graph of <m>q</m>, and <m>(3,-3)</m>
                lies on the graph of <m>p</m>.
            </p>

          <sidebyside widths="40% 35%">
            <tabular>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                <cell>0</cell>
                <cell>1</cell>
                <cell>2</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
                <cell>4</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
                <cell>10</cell>
                <cell>15</cell>
                <cell>20</cell>
                <cell>25</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
                <cell>9</cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
                <cell>2</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>The graphs of p and q for you to combine.</shortdescription>
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-5">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-4,4),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(3.5,-4))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,2.)" alignment="e"><m>p</m></label>

                                <line endpoints="((-4,-4),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((4,1),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <label anchor="(-3,-2.)" alignment="s"><m>q</m></label>

                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
            </image>
          </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>h(x) = f(x)+g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>h(3)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>r(x) = p(x) -q(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>r(-1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> for which <m>r(x) = 0</m>?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>k(x) = f(x) \cdot g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>k(0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>s(x) = \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>.
                    Determine <m>s(1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> in the interval
                    <m>-4\le x \le 4</m> for which <m>s(x)</m> is not defined?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>